Original commit body from 28d3c68a0639addecde28866aafc7d13922c0321:

Records the six decisions WI-tofog asked for, so WI-lakid (projection),
WI-rudat (write-back bridge) and WI-vojil (sync endpoint) have one shape
to build to:

1. One Y.Doc per tracker root. Only title, description and a draft
   discussion entry are live-editable, each committing as the op it
   already is (update --title / --description, discuss). Status,
   priority, tags, links, fields, locked_fields, frozen and posted
   entries are op-only plain values. Stealth items are not projected:
   serve exists to be reached from a phone, and stealth never leaves the
   machine.
2. The ops log wins. A live edit is real only once the bridge emits its
   op through TrackerSet/Store (never an .ops write); commit on 5 s idle
   or an explicit post, not per keystroke; an op landing on a field with
   uncommitted live text supersedes it and the editor is told, not
   silently re-applied.
3. Pseudonymous Yjs clientID in the doc; the token -> by/actor table in
   the server process, filled at authenticated handshake; unknown tokens
   refused.
4. Awareness carries a server-assigned pseudonym and kind, no identity
   (PlazaFlow v1.2 §4.8.5).
5. Yjs 13.6 (14 is still pre-release on npm as of 2026-10-29), pycrdt
   0.14.10 + pycrdt-websocket 0.16.5, on a /yjs route inside create_app's
   WI-hopip guard; a Node y-websocket sidecar is rejected. States the
   dependency on WI-pupod (run_server wires no TrackerSet), not raising it.
6. 127.0.0.1 only until WI-sunan; no encryption (WI-pufun); not
   federation; not a replacement for tracker sync.

"Why not BlockSuite" is cited from ADR-0019 rather than restated.

